# How do you read a 12 GB register file without crashing your machine?
prompt: "Why does pd.read_csv blow up memory, and what do dtype, category and usecols do to the bill? When does it pay to switch from CSV to parquet, and what does the conversion cost? How do you read the file in chunks without losing patients whose rows straddle two chunks?"
next: {"title":"What actually links a hospital contact to a prescription?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/registerdata-i-praksis-python-og-pandas/hva-er-det-egentlig-som-binder-en.cast"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

## When npr.csv is 12 GB
lang: en
voice: female
level: advanced
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - File on disk
      - DataFrame
      - Peak
      - Machine RAM
    values: '{regnskap.frames}'
    stage: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 55
    value_labels: true
    y_label: Gigabytes
    title: 'npr.csv: 12 GB, 90M contact rows'

    ```brython regnskap font_size 15 show code hidden true
    n = 90000000  # contact rows
    def gb(b): return round(n*b/1e9, 1)
    def obj(t): return gb(8+49+t) # str
    text = 2*obj(10) + obj(5) + obj(12)
    numeric = gb(8) + gb(9*8)  # pid+9
    ram = round(text + numeric, 1)
    peak = round(ram * 1.5, 1) # buffers
    frames = [[12,0,0,16],[12,ram,peak,16]]
    ```

Why does a twelve-gigabyte register file kill a machine with sixteen gigabytes of RAM? This part does the accounting — and shows why your cohort dies in a MemoryError before a single line of analysis.
    draw title axes

Our NPR extract — from the Norwegian Patient Registry — is one file of twelve gigabytes with ninety million contact rows, one row per stay or visit.
    draw regnskap regnskap_line_1 parallel true

Twelve gigabytes on disk on the left, sixteen gigabytes of RAM in the machine on the right. On paper it fits — and that is exactly why people try.
    draw bar_1 bar_4

    pause 0.4

But the bill is paid in memory: an int64 costs eight bytes per row, while a string in object dtype costs an eight-byte pointer plus a whole Python object of about forty-nine bytes — before a single character is counted.
    draw regnskap_line_2 regnskap_line_3

In pandas 2, dato_inn, dato_ut, icd10 and contact type become object unless you say otherwise: four text columns, almost twenty-four gigabytes by pandas' own deep count.
    draw regnskap_line_4

pid and the nine numeric columns take 7.2 gigabytes together — they are not the problem here.
    draw regnskap_line_5

Thirty-one gigabytes for the DataFrame itself, and the parser's buffers sit on top while reading, so the peak is about one and a half times that.
    draw regnskap_line_6 regnskap_line_7

Two steps in the accounting: before read_csv, and after.
    draw regnskap_line_8 bar_2 bar_3

As soon as pd.read_csv gets the file, it grows to thirty-one gigabytes in memory, with a peak of forty-six and a half.
    animate stage 1 delivery grave duration 3

The machine has sixteen. The accounting doesn't care.
    highlight bar_4 effect glow

    quiz "Why does the icd10 column cost 5.6 GB when the code is only five characters?" intro "A quick check."
        right: In object dtype every cell is its own PyObject with a pointer and overhead — the characters themselves are under ten percent.
        wrong: The characters are small change; it's the per-cell object overhead that costs.
        * Five characters times ninety million rows
        + Every cell is its own Python string object with about 57 bytes of overhead
        * pandas reads the column as float64

And the E11 cohort only needs pid, dato_inn and icd10 — three of fourteen columns. You pay full price for the other eleven and throw them away.
    focus bar_2 bar_3

The process also grows for minutes before it hits the ceiling, so you have time to fetch a coffee and believe it worked.
    point at.ref bar_3 gesture tap

The ratio here is almost four times the file size — the rule of thumb of three to five holds, and the more text your columns hold, the higher in that range you land.
    highlight bar_1 bar_3 effect pulse

    quiz "How much RAM should you plan for a 12 GB CSV read naively with read_csv?"
        right: Three to five times the file size is the rule of thumb, and the share of text columns decides where in that range you land.
        wrong: pandas compresses nothing in memory — the representation grows larger than the file, not smaller.
        * About 12 GB
        + 3–5 times the file size
        * Half — pandas keeps it compressed

The file isn't too big: the representation pandas picks when you say nothing is — and that you can override.
    highlight bar_2 effect glow

The fixes all start from this bill: dtype, category and usecols shrink it, parquet beats CSV, and reading in chunks — without losing a single patient — keeps the peak down.

